\section{Introduction}

The prediction of time series is a topic of extreme relevance in virtually all economic and financial settings.
Therefore, both academic and private researchers aim to devise more effective, reliable and, in the now-common cases where data is extremely abundant, parsimonious methods and models to forecast. Specifically, in the field of financial portfolio management, the prediction of stock features (such as price, return, volatility, and trading volume) is a key component of many trading strategies. Traditionally, it is common to rely on linear models for this purpose, as they are straightforward to implement and analyze. 
In recent years, great strides in the development of machine learning (ML) and statistical learning methods have much expanded the universe of possible forecasting approaches. Therefore, a great variety of techniques have been proposed to improve the prediction of stock market data \citep{kumbure2022machine}. Many efforts involving neural networks (NNs) date back to at least the 1990s \citep{zhangForecastingArtificialNeural1998}, see e.g. \cite{refenes1994stock,ghosn1996multi,saad1998comparative}. 
In recent years, there have been works studying financial machine learning forecasting from both empirical \citep{sirignanoUniversalFeaturesPrice2019,aletiIntradayMarketReturn2025} and theoretical perspectives. Importantly, from the latter point of view, works such as \cite{didisheimComplexityFactorPricing2023} and \cite{kellyVirtueComplexityReturn2024} have formally argued why and how more complex models can much improve prediction, the so-called ``virtue of complexity'' \citep{kellyVirtueComplexityReturn2024}.

In the systematic trading industry, it has also become more common to rely on multi-period portfolio construction approaches to solve for optimal buy and sell decisions of financial assets \citep{meucci2016dynamic}. 
This is especially true in a mid-frequency trading context, where an investment manager combines diverse signals, each of which may be better suited at predicting either shorter or longer horizon returns.\footnote{The systematic trading literature refers to this as having a different \emph{alpha decay profile}.} 
Multi-period portfolio optimization is a natural extension of the mean-variance optimization (MVO) model developed by \cite{markowitz1952portfolio}. The traditional MVO approach is based on a single-period model. The goal of the multi-period mean-variance optimization is, in contrast, to dynamically allocate capital to financial assets by considering important intertemporal aspects such as rebalancing costs, trading impacts, time-varying constraints, price trends, and more.  
While the focus of this work is primarily on forecasting, multi-period portfolio optimization more generally is a well-studied topic in finance \citep{blake2003theRightTool,grinold2007dynamic,qian2007informationHorizon,grinold2010signal,sneddon2008theTortoise,garleanu2013dynamic}, and we contribute to this literature by showing how predictability changes at different intraday horizons of a large cross-section of stocks.

This paper aims to follow both veins of the financial time series literature by proposing an efficient nonlinear methodology to forecast returns at intraday frequencies and multiple horizons. We consider a special class of recurrent-type neural networks (RNNs) which are trained so that the internal state parameters are fixed at their random initialization values. To be precise, we be working with so-called \textit{Echo State Network} (ESN) models \citep{jaeger2001echo,lukoseviciusReservoirComputingApproaches2009}. 
When using a linear readout layer, this class of models can be fitted to data by means of regularized linear regression, a much simpler learning algorithm in comparison to the computationally intensive procedures used by (deep) neural networks. 
Our methodology builds on the recent advances in using ESNs for macroeconomic forecasting at different horizons and frequencies \citep{Ballarin2024reservoirComputingMacro}. The choice of applying the Echo State Networks to an intraday systematic trading context is also motivated by the empirical success ESNs have had in prediction tasks attempted in other fields \citep{tanakaRecentAdvancesPhysical2019}. It is also motivated by three properties ESNs have and are useful in this framework: (i) the \textit{expressivity} of Echo State Network models, which are universal in terms of approximating nonlinear time series models; (ii) ESN adaptability to \emph{multi-horizon forecasting} by virtue of a nonlinear state formulation; (iii) the \emph{computational advantage} of ESNs, which allows solving a high-dimensional data problem in a short processing time.  

We consider two linear baseline models: A linear regression over a vector of signals based on past market and stock performance, and a regularized linear model that uses ridge regression over the same set of signals. Our proposed novel ESN approach is predicated instead on first constructing a recurrent state equation that nonlinearly combines signals over time. The target returns are then predicted with a linear function of the ESN states, again using ridge-regularized least squares. 
For our regularized models, we employ a within-day rolling window cross-validation strategy to select hyperparameters. This setup allows us to systematically evaluate the performance gains of including regularization in the linear model, and additionally those obtained as a result of the ESN model. 

In our application, we target 500 US stock price returns across 5 different intraday horizons at 10-minute intervals each day within the trading session. To measure forecasting effectiveness, we consider both mean squared forecasting error (MFSE) and the $R^2$ of predicted returns. 
Our results show that our ESN-based forecasting strategy for the cross-section of returns dominates the benchmarks at all intraday horizons, with only the case of end-of-day (close) returns showing rather modest improvements. 
Notably, we achieve an up to 0.87\% MSFE reduction that is also remarkably robust to the inherent model sampling randomness of ESN models.
Per-horizon Diebold-Mariano and model confidence set (MCS) tests confirm that these gains are all statistically significant. 
Finally, our full forecasting pipeline can be run over one calendar year of data in a handful of minutes, highlighting the numerical efficiency of our ESN proposal.

\paragraph{Outline.}

In Section~\ref{section:literature}, we provide a review of the recent literature on ML methods in finance.
Precise descriptions of the data, trading setting, and benchmark models are given in Section~\ref{section:data_implement}. 
Section~\ref{section:esn_models} presents our proposed ESN model to forecast stock returns based on user-provided signals (we shall also use the word ``features'' interchangeably), as well as training procedures.
Section~\ref{section:application}, where we discuss our application to US stock returns prediction over the year 2013, follows.
Finally, Section~\ref{section:conclusion} concludes.


\section{Related Literature}\label{section:literature}

Classical mathematical finance suggests that raw stock returns are unpredictable given past information. Yet, recent empirical research has shown this not to be entirely the case at intraday frequencies. For example, \cite{ait-sahalia2022how} provide extensive evidence of predictability at millisecond scales. 
We tackle a more moderate setting, with data sampled at most at 10-minute intervals, and find that at this coarser scale, too, there seems to be non-negligible predictability.\footnote{In terms of \textit{volatility} forecasting, \cite{dhaene2020incorporating} show that intraday (5-minute resolution) and overnight returns can also be incorporated in multivariate GARCH, BEKK-type models to considerably sharpen prediction.} 

There are many works in past two decades that implement various machine learning techniques in a financial setting.
\cite{olson2003neural} and \cite{kwon2007aHybrid} both employ modern neural network approaches to stock forecasting.
\cite{yu2020stock} more recently employed {Deep} Neural Networks (DNNs) for the same task, finding that the flexibility afforded by deep models improves performance.
\cite{borovkova2019anEnsembleLSTM} use a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) recurrent architecture to classify stocks in high-frequency.
\cite{tolo2020predicting} exploits RNN-type networks in order to predict systemic crises in the financial sector.
With a view towards the structural changes due to the COVID-19 pandemic, \cite{chandra2021bayesian} resort to a Bayesian NN approach to forecast prices of stocks from exchanges in the United States, Germany, China, and Australia, while \cite{abedin2021deep} use a more standard deep learning approach to perform exchange rate prediction. 
Further, \cite{wang2024factorGAN} propose a factor-based Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) to tackle both prediction and portfolio investment problems.
For the purposes of MVO specifically, \cite{kim2024enhancing} also applied GANs: Their idea is to perform anomaly detection, and augment the portfolio optimization problem with said information. 
\cite{vuleticFinGANForecastingClassifying2024}, using a novel ``Fin-GAN'' model, have shown that GANs trained with an economics-driven loss can be effective in achieving higher Sharpe ratios compared to both LSTM and ARIMA models.
Lastly, it is important to mention that ML models, such as GANs and Variational Autoencoders, have also been extensively studied for the purpose of synthetic financial data generation, see e.g. \cite{kim2023aGANs-Based,masi2023onCorrelated,nagy2023generative,acciaio2024time,kwon2024can}, and \cite{cetingoz2025synthetic} for a theoretical critique of this line of research.

Complex neural network models are, by nature, difficult to implement. Echo State Networks are a recent development in the field of machine learning models that aim to tackle this issue, while still enjoying the broad effectiveness of neural networks.
ESNs have been applied to a wide variety of contexts \citep{sunSystematicReviewEcho2024}. For example, ESNs have been successfully employed to predict water levels \citep{coulibaly2010reservoirGreatLakes}, electricity loads \citep{deihimi2012echoStateElectricityLoad}, and power generation by renewable sources \citep{hu2020forecastingEnergyConsumptionDeepESN}. Variations involving deep architectures have also been devised \citep{kim2020deepESNTimeSeries}.
In the context of economic forecasting, \cite{Ballarin2024reservoirComputingMacro} constructed a class of ESN models that can incorporate data sampled at different frequencies, achieving state-of-the-art performance when forecasting US GDP growth.
However, the literature on financial applications is, to the best of our knowledge, minimal. \cite{liu2018ESNFinancialDataForecasting} provide an ESN application to financial data, but primarily in the context of hyperparameter optimization, while \cite{trierweilerribeiroNovelHybridModel2021} use ESN models for the prediction of stock return volatilities. Other models based on randomization have also been considered, but this subject is arguably still in its infancy; see e.g. \cite{akyildirim2023randomized} for \textit{random signatures} applied to portfolios and \cite{gonon2023random} for theoretical guarantees on \textit{random shallow network} learning of Black-Scholes PDEs.


\section{Data and Setup}\label{section:data_implement}

Intraday stock data is sourced from AlgoSeek, a market-leading platform providing high-quality high-frequency trading data.\footnote{\url{https://www.algoseek.com/}} 
The data provider provides access to 1-minute resolution OHLC (open, high, low, close price) bars built from the reported Trades of the SIP (consolidated) data feed, which includes all U.S. Exchanges and FINRA from January 2007 to October 2020.
AlgoSeek bars cover the entire trading day:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Pre-Market: 4:00:00~AM to 9:29:59~AM.
    \item Market: 09:30:00~AM to 4:00:00~PM.
    \item Extended hours: 4:00:01~PM to 8:00:00~PM.
\end{itemize}

Throughout our analysis, we employ Eastern Standard Time (EST) data time-stamps, although actual time and date may be different for each exchange due to geographical location. Due to the large volume of data associated with 1-minute frequency, we downsample observations to 10-minute resolution in our empirical exercises.
For an extended description of the data and our preprocessing, we refer the reader to Appendix~\ref{appendix:data}.

\subsection{Trading Setting}

The design of the forecasting experiment reproduces the setup of an intraday trading book, which rebalances its position only on an intraday basis and does not keep open the positions overnight. Accordingly, we assume that a portfolio rebalance takes place every 10 minutes, from 9:30~AM to 3:50~PM. 
Until 3:50~PM, we run individual stock return predictions every 10 minutes. It is important to note that all our predictions are done over future \emph{overlapping} return horizons. For example, at 9:30~AM, the 10-minute prediction horizon goes from the 9:31~AM price bar to the 9:41~AM price bar. On the other hand, the 30-minute prediction horizon goes from the 9:31~AM price bar to the 10.01~AM price bar. 

We target the following prediction horizons:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{10 minutes} \textnormal{(10min)}: 39 predictions per day over this horizon.\footnote{We count the number of 10-minute intervals from 9:30 AM to 3:50 PM, which is the last time-stamp available for an intraday 10-minute horizon prediction. At 3:50 PM, the targeted return takes the closing auction price into account for the return calculation.}
    \item \emph{30 minutes} \textnormal{(30min)}: 37 predictions per day over this horizon.%
    \item \emph{60 minutes} \textnormal{(60min)}: 34 predictions per day.%
    \item \emph{2 hours} \textnormal{(2hr)}: 28 predictions per day.%
    \item \emph{End-of-day} \textnormal{(\textsc{eod})}: 39 predictions per day. Specifically, \textsc{eod} returns are built from the price at a particular time-stamp and the daily closing price.%
\end{itemize}

Given this framework, we do not carry out any return prediction at 4:00~PM, but rather we assume using the auction to fully exit our book positions, submitting our orders at 3:50~PM to be executed at the closing auction price. Accordingly, we do not target any continuous session close to auction and overnight return horizons.



\subsection{Modeling Setup}\label{subsection:data_setup}

Formally, we let the universe of tradeable stocks be indexed by $i = 1, \ldots, N$ so that this index does not change over time. For each stock, we have access to both future returns, $r^{(i)}_{t+h}$ collected in a $D$-dimensional vector $Z^{(i)}_{t} = (Z^{(i)}_{1,t}, \ldots, Z^{(i)}_{D,t} )' \in \Real^{D}$. We shall also assume that $D$ is fixed over time.
Note that $r^{(i)}_{t+h}$ is indexed by horizon $h$, which for our purposes is an arbitrary intraday interval of time.
For example, we let $h = \textnormal{30min}$ be the current 30-minute-ahead return, and $h = \textnormal{\textsc{eod}}$ to indicate the current end-of-day return.
If the $i$-th stock return or any of its signals are not observed at time $t$, we discard it for period $t$ in our empirical exercise.\footnote{Numerically, if a stock drops from the panel at a certain date or data is missing, we use \texttt{NaN}s to represent its signals and/or returns for that time index. 
} 
This means that the observed instantaneous stock cross-section has size $N_t \leq N$.

Our per-horizon forecasting objective is the prediction of the conditional expected return 
\begin{equation*}
    \E\big[ r^{(i)}_{t+h} \,\big\vert\, \mathcal{F}^{(i)}_t \big] ,
    \quad\text{where}\quad
    \mathcal{F}^{(i)}_t := \sigma(Z^{(i)}_t, Z^{(i)}_{t-1}, \ldots)
\end{equation*}
and $\sigma(Z^{(i)}_t, Z^{(i)}_{t-1}, \ldots)$ is the sigma algebra generated by all past realizations of $Z^{(i)}_t$, that is, the history of stock-specific signals. We are going to assume throughout that $r^{(i)}_{t,h}$ is measurable with respect to this filtration, and that any observable predictive information associated to past returns is already fully embedded in the signals.

\subsection{Signal Construction}
\label{subsection:signals}

Our primary goal in signal construction is to be able to separate stock-specific features from general market structure. Moreover, while we could, in principle, work with discrete signals, such as e.g. buy-to-open or sell-to-open, and combinations of these with continuous signals, our ESN models are intrinsically adapted to continuous inputs. 
Therefore, we focus on the systematic construction of real-valued signals following \cite{avellanedaStatisticalArbitrageUS2010}. We discuss in more detail the technical aspects of the construction of signals in Appendix~\ref{section:signal_construction}. 

Let $P^{(i)}_t$ be the individual asset price. First, assume that prices are determined as a continuous-time stochastic process. Returns can be decomposed into drift, market-factor (systematic) and idiosyncratic components,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:factor_model}
    \frac{\d P^{(i)}_t}{P^{(i)}_t}
    =
    a^{(i)} \d t + \sum_{j=1}^J b^{(i)}_{j} F_{j,t} + \d U^{(i)}_t ,
\end{equation}
where $a^{(i)}$ is the stock price drift, $\{ F_{j,t} \}_{j=1}^J$ are the returns of $J$ risk-factors associated to the market, $\{ b^{(i)}_{j} \}_{j=1}^J$ are the $J$ factor loadings and $\d U^{(i)}_t$ is a stock-specific residual component. 
We then model the residual term as an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:OU_process}
    \d U^{(i)}_t 
    = 
    \kappa^{(i)} \big( m^{(i)} - U^{(i)}_{t} \big) \d t + \sigma^{(i)} \d W_{t} ,
\end{equation}
where $W_{t}$ is the standard Wiener process.
As in \cite{avellanedaStatisticalArbitrageUS2010}, $\kappa^{(i)}$, $m^{(i)}$, and $\sigma^{(i)}$ are stock-specific parameters which are only slowly varying over time. 

The return residual model given by \eqref{eq:factor_model}-\eqref{eq:OU_process} can then be treated in discrete time and estimated using two steps. First, applying principal component analysis (PCA) the market factors are extracted from observed returns, and thus regression
\begin{equation*}
    r^{(i)}_t 
    =
    a^{(i)} + \sum_{j=1}^J b^{(i)}_{j} F_{j,t} + \upsilon^{(i)}_t
\end{equation*}
allows one to construct market-factors residuals $\{ \widehat{\upsilon}^{(i)}_t \}$. Then, for a chosen discretization window size $P > 0$, we can introduce the discretized residuals $\widehat{U}^{(i)}_{P,t} := \sum_{s=t-P}^{t} \widehat{\upsilon}^{(i)}_s$. This, in turn, enables the second step, wherein we perform estimation of the OU process by means of an AR(1) regression,
\begin{equation*}
    \widehat{U}^{(i)}_{P,t+1} = c_0^{(i)} + c_u^{(i)} \widehat{U}^{(i)}_{P,t} + \eta^{(i)}_{P,t} ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\kappa^{(i)}_P := -\log(c_u^{(i)})$ and $m^{(i)}_P := c_0^{(i)} / (1 - c_u^{(i)})$ are windowed estimates of $\kappa^{(i)}$ and $m^{(i)}$, respectively.
Thus, we can define the \textit{z-score signal} with window size $P$ as 
\begin{equation*}
    z^{(i)}_{P,t} := \frac{\widehat{U}^{(i)}_{P,t} - m^{(i)}_P}{\sigma^{(i)}_P} ,
    \quad \text{where} \quad
    \sigma^{(i)}_P := \sqrt{ \frac{\Var(\eta^{(i)}_{P,t})}{2 \kappa^{(i)}_P} } .
\end{equation*}
In practice, as the returns process \eqref{eq:factor_model} includes a drift $a^{(i)}$, we construct a \textit{modified z-score signal} defined by 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mod-z-score_signal}
    \tilde{z}^{(i)}_{P,t} := z^{(i)}_{P,t} - \frac{a^{(i)}}{\kappa^{(i)}_P \sigma^{(i)}_P} ,
\end{equation}
which now incorporates the stock-specific drift component into the signal.

In our application, we estimate a total of 15 market-factors.\footnote{To handle missing price observations, we simply forward-fill return data, which is equivalent to the standard assumption that when prices are unobserved, they remain fixed to the last observation. In total, the factors we use account for more than 90\% of the variability of observed returns.} 
We build a total of six signals (stacked in vector $Z^{(i)}_t$) that differ in the level of discretization $P$. 
A core signal is computed following \eqref{eq:mod-z-score_signal} and setting  $P = 10$. We additionally built five alternative signals, also following \eqref{eq:mod-z-score_signal}, which differ from the core signal due to setting $P \in \{20, 30, 60, 100, 150\}$.

\subsection{Linear Return Prediction}\label{subsection:linear_model}

After signal construction, to establish a prediction baseline, we consider a direct linear forecasting model, where future returns are predicted as an affine function of signals,
\begin{equation*}
    r^{(i)}_{t+h} = \mu_{t,h} + \beta_{t,h}' Z^{(i)}_t + \epsilon^{(i)}_{t+h} ,
\end{equation*}
where $\mu_{t,h}$ is the market's average return, $\beta_{t,h} \in \Real^{D}$ is the vector of feature coefficients, which is, in general, horizon and time-dependent, and $\epsilon^{(i)}_{t+h}$ is an error term. We make the standard assumption that $\beta_{t,h}$ does not vary along the cross-section, that is, the signals we construct correctly embed any stock-specific information. The linear return prediction is thus $\widehat{r}^{(i)}_{t+h} = \mu_{t,h} + \beta_{t,h}' Z^{(i)}_t$. 
We discuss estimation of coefficients $\mu_{t,h}$ and $\beta_{t,h}$ in Section~\ref{subsection:training}.

\section{Echo State Networks}\label{section:esn_models}

The idea that random parameters may work well in complex nonlinear models such as artificial neural networks goes back a long way, see e.g. \cite{lowe1988multivariableFunctionalInterp,Schmidt1992feedforwardNeuralRandom}. Different research fields have expanded on this concept:
\cite{huang2006universalApproxRandom} proposed the ``extreme learning machine'' form for shallow NNs with randomly sampled internal weights;\footnote{By \textit{shallow} we intend here that the number of hidden layers of the network is small, usually only one. However, neural networks with even a single layer are effective functional approximators. Textbook discussions of such results can be found e.g. in \cite{hastie2009theElements}, and also in \cite{anthony2009neuralNetwork} or \cite{zhang2023mathematical} for formal theory.}
\cite{rahimi2008UniformApproximationFunctions,rahimi2008randomKitchenSinks} developed the ``random kitchen sinks'' approach (i.e. collections of functions with random parameters) in the setting of reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces (RKHS). More recent work on determining the broad success of deep neural networks has looked into the ``lottery ticket'' hypothesis \citep{frankleLotteryTicketHypothesis2018,malachProvingLotteryTicket2020,maSanityChecksLottery2021}, which informally states that in large NNs there is a high chance that a small subnetwork performs almost as well as its much larger super-network -- even at initialization, \textit{before} coefficients are trained \citep{sreenivasanRareGemsFinding2022}. Works such as  \cite{zhaoZerOInitializationInitializing2022} and \cite{bolagerSamplingWeightsDeep2023} go further and design special sampling schemes that try to optimize the initial weight sampling strategy, so that negligible-to-zero training is needed.
In tandem, \textit{reservoir computing} (RC) models have been developed as a (broad) family of methods which \textit{explictly} embrace that idea that most model parameters do not need to be trained, but can instead be randomly drawn from some distributions chosen by the researcher, independently of the data \citep{lukoseviciusPracticalGuideApplying2012}.\footnote{We remark that, while the coefficients are sampled at random, this does not mean that the models' \textit{architecture} is also necessarily random. Indeed, taking into account e.g. time dependencies is important.} 
Reservoir models more particularly originate from the fields of dynamic system analysis and control theory.
An overarching subfamily of interest is thus that of general random-weights neural networks (RWNN), which encompasses all NN-like model designs where at least some of the weights are drawn randomly and not trained.

As mentioned in the introduction, an important consideration pertaining RC and RWNN methods is their \textit{computational efficiency}. Artificial neural network training often demands significant, if not extreme, investments related to both physical resources (computer clusters, hardware, etc.) and energy; these costs have been steadily rising over the past years as models quickly become more complex \citep{Lohn2022aiComputeHowMuchLonger}. Since RC and RWNN models do not require full parameter training, the costs to optimize such models can be orders of magnitude lower. This also means that they can be effectively deployed in contexts where systematic re-training (e.g. online learning) is required.


\subsection{ESN Model Formulation}

Echo State Networks are a relatively recent development in the field of nonlinear time series modeling. They belong to both reservoir computing and random weights networks families. They are architecturally analogous to recurrent neural networks (RNN), in that they explicitly model time-dependence of data using a state equation.

Formally, suppose that time series $Y_t$ and $Z_t$ are observed, and we seek to predict $Y_t$ using $Z_t$ and its past realizations (history). In our setting, $Z_t$ is a vector of stock-specific signals, while $Y_t$ are stock returns.
Let $Z_t = (Z_{1,t}, \ldots, Z_{D,t} )' \in \Real^D$, where $D \geq 1$ is the number of stock signals, and $Y_t \in \Real$.
An ESN model maps features $Z_t$ to a \textit{recurrent state} $X_t$ defined by the equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:esn_state}
    X_t := \alpha X_{t-1} + (1-\alpha) \varphi\big( A X_t + C Z_t + b \big) ,
\end{equation}
where $X_t \in \Real^K$, for $K \geq D$; $A \in \Real^{K \times K}$, $C \in \Real^{K \times D}$ and $b \in \Real^{K}$ are the state equation coefficients; $\varphi : \Real \mapsto \Real$ is a nonlinear, element-wise activation function (e.g. hyperbolic tangent or ReLU), and $\alpha \in [0,1]$ is the state leak hyperparameter. Note that in the special setting where $K = D$, $\varphi(x) = x$, $A = 0_D$ is the $D\times D$ zero matrix, $C = I_D$ is the identity matrix of size $D$, $b = 0$ is the zero vector and $\alpha = 0$ the above state equation simplifies to a linear case, i.e. $X_t \equiv Z_t$. Therefore, by introducing state $X_t$ we are considering a strictly more general class than that of linear models. The final output of the ESN is determined by a linear layer,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:esn_output}
    X_t \:\mapsto\: \mu_t  +  \theta_t' X_t,
\end{equation}
where $\theta_t \in \Real^{K}$ are the output coefficients and $\mu_t \in \Real$ is the output intercept term. Notice here that in the ESN we allow for the output coefficients to depend on time index $t$, while state parameters ($A$, $B$ and $\alpha$) are constant.
The model is fitted by least squares, solving
\begin{equation}\label{eq:esn_least_squares}
    \big\{ \widehat{\mu}_t, \widehat{\theta}_t \big\}
    \: := \: 
    \arg\min_{\mu, \theta} 
    \sum_{t=T_1}^{T_2} \left(
        Y_t - \mu - \theta' X_t 
    \right)^2 ,
\end{equation}
where we have assumed that a data sample over periods $T_1 \leq t \leq T_2$, $T_1 < T_2$, is available.

\begin{remark}
    Theoretical analysis of ESN models provides insight and justification for their properties and their performance. For example, \cite{grigoryeva2018EchoStateNetworksUniversal} and \cite{gonon2020ReservoirComputingUniversality} have shown that ESNs are universal approximators of time filters, meaning that, under some general conditions, if $Y_t = H(Z_t, Z_{t-1}, \ldots)$ for some mapping $H$ from the infinite past history of $Z_t$ to $Y_t$, then an ESN can approximate $H$ arbitrarily well. \cite{gononApproximationBoundsRandom2023} obtained explicit bounds for this approximation problem. Previously, in \cite{gonon2020RiskBoundsReservoir}, the same authors also constructed bounds on the error committed when an ESN model is trained and then applied to new data.
    Such results provide broad theoretical grounding for our choice of ESNs as nonlinear models to apply to financial time series prediction.
\end{remark}

We underline here that simply drawing $A$, $C$, and $b$ from some prescribed distribution will generally not yield an effective ESN model. This is because specific properties, such as the so-called \textit{echo state} and \textit{fading memory} properties \citep{grigoryeva2018EchoStateNetworksUniversal}, are essential in guaranteeing the well-posedness, first, and effectiveness, second, of reservoir models. 
Therefore, we introduce some additional hyperparameters, following the discussion in e.g. \cite{Ballarin2024reservoirComputingMacro}.
One begins by drawing random matrices $A^*$, $C^*$ and $b^*$. These are then normalized according to a specific matrix (pseudo)norm, $\norm{\cdot}_A$, $\norm{\cdot}_C$ and $\norm{\cdot}_b$, respectively, yielding $\overline{A} := A^* / \norm{A^*}_A$, $\overline{C} := C^* / \norm{C^*}_C$ and $\overline{b} := b^* / \norm{b^*}_b$. The state equation then becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:esn_state_explicit}
    X_t := \alpha X_{t-1} + (1-\alpha) \varphi\big( \rho \overline{A}\, X_t + \gamma \overline{C}\, Z_t + \zeta \overline{b}\, \big) ,
\end{equation}
where $\rho \in [0,1]$, $\gamma > 0$, and $\zeta \geq 0$ are the spectral radius, input scaling, and bias scaling, respectively. The reason why $\rho$ is taken to be within the interval $[0,1]$ is that $\norm{\cdot}_A$ is most often chosen to either be the 2-norm, or the largest absolute eigenvalue norm. We use the latter, so that the spectral radius of $\overline{A}$ is unity. 

\begin{remark}
    The way of sampling $A^*$, $C^*$, and $b^*$ is itself a question of high practical importance. Many works have addressed this topic from an applied or computational perspective, see e.g. \cite{lukoseviciusPracticalGuideApplying2012} and reference within. 
    From a theoretical perspective, \cite{gononApproximationBoundsRandom2023} derived explicit distributions for the state parameter matrices that depend on the structure of the approximation problem.
    In this work, we favor a practical perspective and apply some well-studied probability distributions. The entries of $A^*$ are drawn from a sparse Gaussian distribution, while the entries of $C^*$ are drawn from a sparse uniform distribution, since some empirical success with these has been observed in \cite{Ballarin2024reservoirComputingMacro}. Moreover, in all of our ESN models, $b$ is set to be the zero vector, without any random draw. Accordingly, we do not discuss the tuning of $\zeta$ in our application.
\end{remark}


\subsection{Training and Estimation}\label{subsection:training}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{diagrams/diagram_resn.pdf}
    \caption{Overview of the proposed ESN model.}
    \label{fig:diagram_resn}
    \caption*{\small
    \textit{Notes:} The signals vector is loaded into the ESN state equation and combined with the previous state via a nonlinear mapping with randomly-sampled parameters. The prediction equation is linear in the ESN states, allowing for (regularized) least squares estimation. The state parameters do not change when fitting the model, and only $\mu_t$, $\theta_t$ are estimated.}
\end{figure}

Our ESN model is constructed using a common state equation over the cross-section, meaning that, for each $i \in \{1, \ldots, N\}$,
\begin{equation*}
    X^{(i)}_t := \alpha X^{(i)}_{t-1} + (1-\alpha) \varphi\big( \rho \overline{A}\, X^{(i)}_t + \gamma \overline{C}\, Z^{(i)}_t \big) ,
\end{equation*}
where $\varphi$, $A$, $C$, $b$ and $\alpha$ are all fixed and common across $i$. The stock-specific ESN regression equation thus becomes
\begin{equation*}
    r^{(i)}_{t+h} = \mu_{t,h} + \theta_{t,h}' X^{(i)}_t + \varepsilon^{(i)}_{t+h} .
\end{equation*}
We provide a diagram of the flow of information in our ESN model in Figure~\ref{fig:diagram_resn}.
As with the linear model, here too we assume that returns can be predicted from signals with regression coefficients that are common across the stock cross-section.
To handle missing data, we introduce what we term \textit{state decay}, meaning that we replace missing signals with zeros, and continue iterating the state equation forward -- although the associated state is not included in the estimated data.

To train the ESN, our objective is to sequentially minimize the current regularized least squares empirical risk,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:loss_estimation}
    \mathcal{R}_{t,h} (\mu, \theta)
    := 
    \frac{1}{M_t N} \sum_{s=t-\tau_h-M_t}^{t-\tau_h-1} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left(r^{(i)}_{s+h} - \mu - \theta' X^{(i)}_s \right)^2 
        + \theta' \Lambda_{t,h} \theta ,
\end{equation}
which averages over both time indices $t-\tau_h-M_t \leq s \leq t-\tau_h-1$ and stock cross-section.\footnote{In this formula, for notational simplicity, we avoid explicitly considering the case where any signal or return in the training window is missing (\texttt{NaN}). However, it is straightforward to allow for this by simply including an indicator function that is non-zero whenever $r^{(i)}_{s+h} \not= \texttt{NaN}$ \textit{and} $Z^{(i)}_t \not= \texttt{NaN}$.} 
Notice that $\mathcal{R}_{t,h} (\mu, \theta)$ is not written explicitly as a function of ESN state coefficients, as these are \textit{not} subject to training/fitting.
The ridge-type regularization penalty is encoded in a possibly time-varying, horizon-specific positive semi-definite matrix $\Lambda_{t,h}$. Since the optimal penalty may change substantially across both time and horizon, we use cross-validation to construct $\mathcal{R}_{t,h} (\mu, \theta)$ in our empirical studies. A detailed discussion of our implementation can be found in Appendix~\ref{appendix:estimation_details}.

To operationalize \eqref{eq:loss_estimation}, two additional tunable hyperparameters need to be set: $1 \leq M_t \leq t-\tau_h$, the \textit{training window size} for the current horizon, and $0 \leq \tau_h \leq t-1$, the \textit{training window buffer}. These quantities serve two distinct roles. The window size controls how far back the model can go to extract information from past realized signals. A short window means there is a shorter panel of data to use to optimize $\mathcal{R}_{t,h} (\mu, \theta)$, but a long panel may include irrelevant information with respect to current market conditions. Window buffer $\tau_h$ is needed because we want to ensure that in our training we consider the time structure of multi-period returns. If $h = \textnormal{10min}$, then at $t$ we can train using states starting from up to ten minutes ago; but if instead we consider $h = \textnormal{2hr}$, then clearly we can only construct training tuples $\{X^{(i)}_s, r^{(i)}_{s+h}\}$ where $s \leq t-h$, meaning the most recent states we can use are from time $t-\textnormal{2hr}$. If we want to avoid constructing empirical estimation scenario where there is information leakage across time, imposing $\tau_h \geq h$ is necessary. Note that this implies that for horizon \textsc{eod} we are forced to use only observations up to the \textit{previous trading day} are valid for \textsc{eod} returns. These considerations need to be taken into account when considering the change in predictive performance of all methods, as presented in Section~\ref{section:application} below.

\paragraph{Baseline and Benchmark Linear Models.}
While we have formalized the training objective for the ESN, it is straightforward to extend our discussion to the linear model of Section~\ref{subsection:linear_model} by replacing $X^{(i)}_t$ with signal vector $Z^{(i)}_t$ and $\theta_{t,h}$ with $\beta_{t,h}$.

In our experiments, we make a further modeling distinction between a \textit{baseline} and a \textit{benchmark} linear model for predicting returns. This is primarily for conceptual reasons: If we consider the linear specification as distinct from the model estimation strategy, it is clear that batched ridge estimation is a separate refinement. Indeed, by using a rolling batch, we may be able to better capture temporary changes in the return and signal structure, include them in the penalization procedure, and thus improving prediction performance compared to a more na{\"i}ve procedure. 
Thus, in summary, we define two distinct reference methods:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Baseline model:} Linear model defined as in Section~\ref{subsection:linear_model} and estimated with minimal window size ($M = 1$) and without any regularization. The associated risk simplifies to
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{R}_{t,h}^{\textnormal{lin}} (\mu, \beta)
        := 
        \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \left( r^{(i)}_{t-\tau_h-1+h} - \mu - \beta' Z^{(i)}_{t-\tau_h-1} \right)^2 .
    \end{equation*}
    \item \textbf{Benchmark model:} Linear model estimated with the same windowing and regularization schemes as our competing ESN model, following \eqref{eq:loss_estimation}:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{R}_{t,h}^{\textnormal{reg-lin}} (\mu, \beta)
        :=
        \frac{1}{M_t N} \sum_{s=t-\tau_h-M_t}^{t-\tau_h-1} \sum_{i=1}^N \left(r^{(i)}_{s+h} - \mu - \beta' Z^{(i)}_{s} \right)^2
            + \beta' \Lambda_{t,h} \beta .
    \end{equation*}
\end{itemize}
As we see in our experiments, it is the case that baseline and benchmark linear models have very close performance. This, in turn, means that we can more directly assign the improved performance to the nonlinear recurrent design of our ESN model -- not only the training scheme itself.


