\subsection{Results}

First, we consider the mean squared forecasting error (MSFE). At prediction horizon $h$ and time $t$ at which we carry out the prediction, the \textit{instantaneous} MSFE across the $N$ stocks in the investible universe at time $t$ is given by
\begin{equation*}
    \textnormal{MSFE}_{t,h} 
    = 
        \frac{1}{N_t} \sum_{i = 1}^{N_t}(r^{(i)}_{t+h, n} - \widehat{r}^{(i)}_{t+h, n})^2 
    .
\end{equation*}
The lower the MSFE, the better the model's performance in a mean-squared error sense. Over an investment period, we also consider the cumulative MSFE, $\textnormal{cuMSFE}_{t,h} = \frac{1}{t} \sum_{s = 1}^{t} \textnormal{MSFE}_{s,h}$, as well as the total cumulated MSFE at terminal time $T$, that is $\textnormal{MSFE}_{h} \equiv \textnormal{cuMSFE}_{T,h}$.

