\section{Conclusion}\label{section:conclusion}

In this work, we have presented a novel approach to forecast intraday stock returns at different horizons by leveraging ESN models. Our proposed method is nonparametric in nature, and is able to significantly improve prediction performance across different horizons by a significant margin, with the only exception being end-of-day returns.
Our findings suggest that there is potentially much to be gained by considering efficient, nonlinear forecasting models compared even to regularized linear methods.
Lastly, even thought our ESN models inherently rely on randomly-sampled parameters, we have shown that the prediction gains are robust to randomness at both short and medium intraday horizons.

We have not discussed model aggregation or ensembles. This means that there is space to potentially design more sophisticated online model selection schemes that can dynamically weight multiple ESNs to improve prediction and profitability: We leave this idea for potential future research. Moreover, we have constrained our ESN output layer to be linear. This is not a necessity, and it would be interesting to evaluate if including more flexible output layers (e.g. a shallow network) could further improve performance.


