\section{Introduction}
% Ultrasound imaging has long been established as a first-line diagnostic modality across diverse clinical settings, celebrated for its real-time capability, portability, and noninvasive nature \cite{szabo2013diagnostic}. Compared to static B-mode snapshots, dynamic ultrasound sequences convey rich spatio-temporal cues that enable in vivo visualization of physiological phenomena such as blood flow dynamics \cite{10672557}, organ motion \cite{10623885}, and tissue deformation \cite{10659347}. Consequently, accurate segmentation of anatomical structures and pathological regions in dynamic ultrasound videos is vital for clinical tasks, such as disease progression monitoring and image-guided diagnosis.

