\end{figure}


\subsection{FBSDE with only $Z$-coupling}\label{example2}

To further demonstrate our findings, we consider a one-dimensional FBSDE with only $Z$-coupling in the forward equation and no $Y$-coupling. The forward equation, therefore, depends on $(t, X, Z)$, and such an equation is out of the setting considered in \cite{bender2008time}. The system of equations reads as follows
\begin{equation}\label{ex2:fbsde}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
X_t = & x_0-\int_0^t \frac{1}{2} \sin (s+X_s) \cos (s+X_s) (\sin^2 (s+X_s) + Z_s)  \mathrm{d} s   + \int_0^t  \cos (s+X_s)  \mathrm{d} W_s \\
Y_t = & \sin (T+X_T)+\int_t^T Y_s Z_s-\cos \left(s+X_s\right) \mathrm{d} s - \int_t^T Z_s \mathrm{d}  W_s
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
with the solution
\begin{equation}\label{ex2:fbsde_sol}
y(t, x) = \sin (t+x), \quad z(t, x)=\cos ^2(t+x)
\end{equation}
We choose $T=0.25$ and $x_0 = 1.5$ for this example, and the choices of $N$ and $M$ for conducting the experiments are as in Example \ref{example1}.


As we can see from Figure \ref{fig2}, similar conclusions for both algorithms can be drawn as for Example \ref{example1}. Figures \ref{fig2a} and \ref{fig2c} clearly show divergence of the Direct Extension algorithm. Err$(Z)$ significantly increases in the time steps while fixing $M=5$, reaching $10^{-2}$ at $N=32$. Such divergence damages the approximation of $Y$ and eventually leads to an increasing Total Error. Figure \ref{fig2b} again shows superior convergence rates, as in Example \ref{example1}. Together with Figure \ref{fig2d}, one may conclude that the stalling of the convergence in the number of iteration steps may be due to the small number of time steps, and choosing a larger $N$ shall further improve the approximation accuracy.

