Although, the effect of $T_i$ on a turbine wake has been studied extensively, the need to characterize the spectral content of the inflow turbulence has been realized only recently \citep{mao2018far, gupta2019low, vinod2019performance, gambuzza2023influence, hodgson2023effects, bourhis2025impact}. In engineering wake models, the growth of the wake is often assumed to be a function of turbulence intensity alone and the spectral characteristic of the incoming flow is often ignored \citep{gambuzza2023influence}. Low frequencies present in the inflow have been shown to modulate the instantaneous power output of a stand alone turbine \citep{chamorro2013interaction}. There is a newly established consensus that wind turbines do not simply advect the structures present in the freestream as was originally assumed by the dynamic wake meandering model \citep{larsen2008wake}. Instead, a turbine has a dynamic response \textit{i.e.} certain frequencies in the freestream can be amplified in the wake \citep{mao2018far,heisel2018spectral,gupta2019low, hodgson2023effects}. \citet{mao2018far} identified nonlinear optimal perturbations to a wind turbine wake modeled as an actuator disk. They reported that as the inflow perturbation amplitude increased, the optimal perturbation frequency reduced from \rB{$St= 0.64$} to $St=0.25$ for $Re_D=1000$. As $Re_D$ was increased to 3000, the optimal perturbation frequency further reduced to $St=0.16$. These low frequencies fall well within the accepted range of wake meandering frequencies \citep{medici2008measurements, chamorro2013interaction, okulov2014regular}. Beyond $Re=3000$, \citet{mao2018far} noted that the optimal frequency did not change, indicating a possible $Re$ invariance \rA{at all application specific Reynolds numbers}. Similarly, through field experiments, \citet{heisel2018spectral} reported that the wind turbine amplifies a range of frequencies in the inflow yielding spectral peaks in the range $St=0.3-0.4$ in the far wake. \rA{Similarly, larger freestream characteristic length scales were shown to induce a stronger wake meandering and were linked to a faster kinetic energy entrainment from the freestream \citep{blackmore2014influence, vahidi2024influence}. However, these studies modeled the turbine as an actuator disk so, the effect of the tip vortices was not considered. The results of these studies likely hold better in the far wake of a turbine which is better approximated by the wake of an actuator disk. Interestingly, \citet{hodgson2023effects} reported a shorter near wake region and faster wake recovery for shorter integral time scales. These results indicate that the freestream scales may have a different effect in the near and the far wake \citep{hodgson2023effects}. The current study aims to further understand the effect of the freestream characteristic length scales on the near wake of a turbine.}   \\



Unlike several previous works where the statistical properties of the wake were studied in the presence of FST, we primarily focus on the dynamic properties. A series of time-resolved PIV experiments are performed on a wind turbine model exposed to freestream turbulence having different turbulence intensities ($T_i$) and integral length scales ($L_v$). In this work we build on our previous works by including freestream turbulence and we attempt to better understand the following aspects: (a) How does the \rA{energy content at} the dominant frequencies in the near-wake change with freestream turbulence? (b) In the absence of freestream turbulence, \citet{biswas2024effect} found a scaling between the wake meandering frequency and $\lambda$ indicating that wake meandering could be a rotor scale bluff body shedding type instability. How does the nature of wake meandering change in the presence of freestream turbulence? (c) How does freestream turbulence affect the energy exchanges between the various coherent modes? (d) Finally, in all of the above what are the relative roles of $T_i$ and $L_v$? \\


