\section{Methodology}

Particle image velocimetry (PIV) experiments were conducted in the hydrodynamics flume in the Department of Aeronautics at Imperial College London. The flume had a cross-sectional area of $60 \times 60$ cm$^2$ at the operating water depth. The turbine radius ($R$) was 0.1 m and the model was the same as that detailed in \citep{biswas2024effect, biswas2024energy}. The model had a nacelle and tower associated with it such that it
resembled a utility-scale turbine. The free-stream velocity ($U_{\infty}$) was kept constant at 0.2 ms$^{-1}$. Experiments were performed at a single tip speed ratio $\lambda = 6$. The Reynolds number based on the turbine diameter $Re_D$ (where $D$ is the turbine diameter) was $\approx 40000$.  

Different freestream turbulence cases were obtained by using different passive turbulence generating grids similar to that used in our previous studies \citep{kankanwadi2020turbulent, chen2023spatial}. Separate PIV experiments were conducted to characterise the variation of turbulence intensity ($T_i$), integral length scales ($L_v$) and the mean streamwise velocity as a function of streamwise distance from the grids. The details of these experiments can be found in appendix 1. The parametric space (spanned by $T_i$ and $L_v$) in this study is shown in figure \ref{fig:grid_points}(a). Specific symbols are used for certain types of grids. The symbols represent the value of $T_i$ and $L_v$ at hub height, while the error bars show the maximum and minimum values observed within the rotor diameter (see the transverse variations of $T_i$ and $L_v$ in figures \ref{fig:FST_T} and \ref{fig:FST_Lv} respectively in appendix 1). 

\rA{Estimating integral length scales involves inherent intricacies and different methods yield nonidentical results in an inhomogeneous anisotropic turbulent flow as in the present case. For instance, the integral length scales can be obtained by the direct computation of the spatial auto-correlation function or by obtaining integral time scales using the temporal auto-correlation and then invoking Taylor's hypothesis of frozen turbulence. In this work, we follow the former approach. The integral length scales are estimated as $L_v = \int_0^{\hat{r}}R_{21}^{'}dr$, where $R_{21}^{'}$ is the streamwise autocorrelation function of the transverse velocity fluctuations. It is worthwhile mentioning that other transverse or streamwise autocorrelation functions were also tested. It was seen that the transverse autocorrelation function of the streamwise velocity ($R_{12}^{'}$) yielded integral length scales of magnitude similar to that obtained using \rB{$R_{21}^{'}$ whilst,} the streamwise autocorrelation function of the streamwise velocity ($R_{11}^{'}$) yielded larger (up to $2-3$ times) integral length scales. However the autocorrelation functions $R_{21}^{'}$ or $R_{12}^{'}$ more often fell off to zero compared to $R_{11}^{'}$ \rB{which exhibited long range correlations}. Therefore, only the integral length scales calculated using $R_{21}^{'}$ are discussed here. A more detailed discussion on this can be found in appendix 1. Nevertheless, the largest integral length scales we are able to produce here ($<0.15D$ using $R_{21}^{'}$ or $<0.45D$ using $R_{11}^{'}$) are an order of magnitude smaller than the largest length scales a real wind turbine residing in an atmospheric boundary layer can be exposed to \citep{larsen2007dynamic, porte2020wind}. \rB{The current study is thus only able to} explore the effect of short FST length scales on the near wake dynamics of a turbine.   }



The grids also introduce a blockage to the freestream, resulting in an acceleration of the incoming flow. Although the tip speed ratio based on $U_{\infty}$ (denoted as $\lambda_{\infty}$) was kept constant at 6 for all the FST cases, due to the blockage induced increment of the freestream velocity, the effective tip speed ratio ($\lambda_{eff}$) reduced for all the FST cases. $\lambda_{eff}$ for the different cases is shown in figure \ref{fig:grid_points}(b). The symbols represent the values at the geometrical \rB{centreline whilst,} the uncertainties are due to the transverse variation of the mean streamwise velocity. The uncertainties increase with $T_i$ as can be expected. The test cases are divided into 4 subgroups, primarily based on turbulence intensity. Group 1 has negligible $T_i$ while groups 2, 3 and 4 have low, mild and high $T_i$ respectively. This classification was done by comparing $T_i$ as well as the nature of the tip vortex related frequencies observed in the near wake as will be discussed in the upcoming sections. Nevertheless, hard boundaries \rB{likely do not }exist between the adjacent subgroups.    


