\section{Vorticity field}

Instantaneous vorticity fields for 4 cases, \rA{one from} each subgroup (increasing turbulence intensity) are shown in figure \ref{fig:42a} for $\lambda_{\infty}=6$ (see also supplementary videos 1-4). For case 1, the tip vortices undergo merging at $x/D \approx 1.5$ similarly to \rB{the observation} in our earlier study \rA{(in the absence of FST)} through a different set of experiments \citep{biswas2024effect}. Due to the outer fluid being non-turbulent, the wake edge is clearly marked by vorticity in case 1. The solid black line shows the mean wake centreline $y=y_c$ obtained by averaging over instantaneous wake centrelines (the loci of the minima in the instantaneous streamwise velocity profiles). The centreline bends towards the $y<0$ side due to the presence of the tower as was also discussed in previous studies \citep{pierella2017wind, biswas2024effect}. For low $T_i$ (case 2), tip vortices and their merging is still observed, however the trajectories of both the individual and merged vortices appear more erratic similar to the observation of \citet{gambuzza2023influence}. \NB{Large scale structures from the freestream penetrate deep into the wake, close to the wake centreline (see the supplementary video 2)}. As $T_i$ is increased further, the tip vortices break down much closer to the turbine as reported in previous studies \citep{chamorro2009wind, wu2012atmospheric, gambuzza2023influence}. The presence of background turbulence makes it difficult to visually identify the wake edge for both medium and high $T_i$ cases. 



