\section{Effect of freestream turbulence on the important frequencies}

 In order to understand the effect of FST on the \rB{coherent motions at different frequencies}, {the transverse velocity spectra {(obtained using FFT)} in the tip region of the near wake ($x/D=0.5$, $y/D=0.55$) are first shown} in figure \ref{fig:43a}(a-d). The symbols in the top right corner correspond to the different FST cases shown in figure \ref{fig:grid_points}(a). For all the FST cases, the dominant frequency is $3f_r$, where $f_r$ is the turbine's rotational frequency. $3f_r$ therefore denotes the frequency of the tip vortices for a three-bladed turbine. Apart from $3f_r$, $f_r$ is also found to be important and is observed in all cases. \citet{biswas2024effect} reported multiple harmonics of $f_r$ in the tip region in the absence of freestream turbulence. These harmonics up to $6f_r$ and even beyond are clearly observed for case 1. The orange band in the low frequency region $0.15\leq St_D \leq 0.4$ shows the typical frequency range of wake meandering reported in previous studies \citep{medici2008measurements, okulov2014regular, chamorro2013interaction}. Note from figure \ref{fig:43a}(a) that without any freestream turbulence, the spectral energy content in this wake meandering frequency band (WMFB) is negligible in the tip region of the near wake. Contrastingly, for all the FST cases (case 2-4) non-negligible spectral peaks are observed in the WMFB even in the tip region. Especially for case 2a, there is a clear peak at $St_D \approx 0.27$. This difference indicates that the \rA{the physics underpinning} wake meandering for case 1 \rB{are} likely different from \NB{the cases with significant levels of freestream turbulence}. 

  The role of the nacelle in the genesis of wake meandering has recently received some attention \citep{howard2015statistics, biswas2024effect}. \citet{howard2015statistics} argued that the nacelle's vortex shedding provides an important perturbation that can trigger wake meandering in the absence of freestream turbulence. \rA{\citet{biswas2024energy} studied the nature of the wake meandering modes without FST. The wake meandering mode was shown to grow radially with downstream distance and it reached the tip region only after $x\approx 3D$.} This justifies the absence of spectral peaks in the WMFB observed in figure \ref{fig:43a}(a). In the presence of freestream turbulence (cases 2-4), perturbations are available everywhere, unlike case 1 where the nacelle's vortex shedding is the main source of the perturbation \citep{howard2015statistics}. Therefore, with FST, wake meandering is likely to start closer to the turbine. As a result, a non-negligible amount of energy content is present in the WMFB for the FST cases. This will be discussed in more detail in the following paragraphs. 








For low $T_i$ (case 2a), most of the harmonics of $f_r$ are observed in the tip region similar to case 1. However, the amplitudes at these frequencies are weaker except $3f_r$, which shows a slightly stronger peak \rA{(higher energy content)}. This could be due to the fact that the freestream velocity for case 2a was slightly higher due to the blockage caused by the grid (see figure \ref{fig:FST_U} in appendix 1), therefore bringing $\lambda_{eff}$ closer to the optimum tip speed ratio (yielding maximum $C_p$) as can be seen from figure 15b of \citet{biswas2024effect}. For case 3, the harmonics such as $2f_r$ and $4f_r$ are observed, although their amplitudes are significantly diminished. $6f_r$ is no longer observed for case 3. Finally, for the highest $T_i$ case, \rB{no spectral peaks are evident other than at $f_r$ and $3f_r$ within the field of view considered here {($0.5 \leq x/D \leq 5$)}}. These frequencies can however still be present very close to the turbine blades.   


