\rB{\subsection{The nature and amplitude of wake meandering in the presence of FST}}

\label{near}

To elucidate the nature of wake meandering in the presence of FST, {power} spectra are shown in figure \ref{fig:freestream_wake_fft} with and without the turbine. In each figure set, the \rB{left} sub figure shows the spectrum in the freestream where the turbine would be placed. The \rB{right} sub figure shows the spectrum in the turbine wake at a distance of $x/D=0.5$ from the turbine itself. The spectra correspond to the same four cases as discussed previously. The energy content in the freestream spectra is the lowest for the no grid case and it increases for the high $T_i$ cases as expected. For all the cases in the turbine wake, low frequency peaks can be observed in the central region (near $y=0$) that correspond to frequencies associated with the nacelle's vortex shedding ($f_n$) and wake meandering ($f_{wm}$). For the no grid case, no signature of wake meandering is found in the wake near the tip region. {If looked at more closely, some non-negligible energy content can be observed in the tip region for the cases 3 and 4b. Interestingly, the low $T_i$ case (case 2a) showed the strongest signature of wake meandering in the tip region. When considering the freestream spectrum for case 2a, a significant energy content can be observed in a similar frequency range and at a similar spatial location (see the green arrow in figure \ref{fig:freestream_wake_fft}(b)). It appears that these frequencies in the freestream are amplified in the turbine wake. The $f_{wm}$ peak in the tip region was nearly 6 times larger than the strongest peak in the same frequency range available within the freestream for case 2a. Further, it seems that the turbine does not equally amplify all frequencies since energy is available over a broader frequency range in the freestream, while in the wake, \rA{the frequency band is narrower}. \rB{A similar amplification of the freestream frequencies was also observed for the case 2b (not shown here for brevity). These observations are in line with those from recent works which suggest that the turbine has a dynamic response \citep{mao2018far, heisel2018spectral, gupta2019low} implying some frequencies in the freestream can have a greater impact in altering the wake dynamics, or in other words, some frequencies may promote stronger wake meandering. For the medium or high $T_i$ cases although a significant energy content is present in the freestream in the WMFB, the amplification of wake meandering is weaker.} \rB{It is worthwhile to note that FST can also indirectly induce/influence wake meandering through breaking down the tip vortices \citep{howard2015statistics}.} Assessing the relative importance of FST in promoting wake meandering directly or indirectly through the tip vortices is non trivial and deserves future investigations. Nevertheless, some evidence of interaction between the tip vortices and wake meandering in the presence of FST is presented in the next paragraph.}  


