\section*{Apendix 1: Grid characterisation}

\label{app1}

 A separate experimental campaign was conducted to study the effect of freestream turbulence on the wind turbine wake. To generate freestream turbulence, 4 different passive turbulence generating grids were used. These are shown in figure \ref{fig:grids}. Among these two were regular grids and two were fractal grids. The flow fields behind these grids were first characterised using PIV experiments. 2 Phantom v641 cameras were used simultaneously to give a stitched field of view length $\approx 2.45D$ ($49 $cm). The FOV was $\approx 18$cm $(0.9D)$ wide and the centre of the FOV coincided with the centre of the flume (\textit{i.e.} $30$cm from the bottom wall). The grid was moved upstream keeping the optical setup intact and a number of non-synchronous experiments were conducted for a particular grid to obtain time averaged statistics over a certain streamwise distance as required. As an example the mean velocity field and the turbulence intensity field behind the grid SRG38 is shown in figure \ref{fig:SRG38}. \NB{Note that just behind the grid, there is a significant amount of acceleration. The pressure drop across the grid reduces the water depth slightly, hence continuity yields the acceleration.} Although the acceleration reduces with downstream distance, the local mean velocity is slightly greater than $U_{\infty}$ even at the farthest location measured. This increment in the freestream velocity needs to be taken into account as it can result in slight changes to the tip speed ratio of the turbine. The turbulence intensity is the highest ($\approx 14\%$) near the grid and monotonically reduces to $\approx 3.5\%$ further downstream. 




The variation of the mean streamwise velocity behind all the grids is shown in figure \ref{fig:FST_U}. The solid black line shows the variation of the mean centreline velocity (along $y=0, z=0$). The jumps in the variation (especially for SFG7) are due to the fact that mean data from multiple non-synchronous PIV experiments were stitched together. The streamwise mean velocity also varies along the $y$ direction. Therefore, the transverse variation of the mean velocity is indicated by the shaded region. The grid is located at $x/D=0$. The dashed vertical lines show the position of the wind turbine in different experiments. Similarly, the variation of $T_i$ behind the grids is shown in figure \ref{fig:FST_T}. 



