{Another important quantity to characterise the nature of freestream turbulence is the integral length scale ($L_v$). $L_v$ is estimated using equation \ref{integral}.} 


\begin{equation}
    R_{21}^{'} (\textbf{x},r) = \frac{\overline{u_2'(\textbf{x}) u_2'(\textbf{x}+r \textbf{e}_1)}} {\sqrt{\overline{u_2'(\textbf{x})^{2}}} \sqrt{\overline{u_2'(\textbf{x}+r \textbf{e}_1)^{2}}}}  \hspace{2 cm} L_v = \int_0^{\hat{r}}R_{21}^{'}dr
    \label{integral}
\end{equation}








{Here $ R_{21}^{'}$ represents the streamwise autocorrelation function of transverse velocity fluctuation ($u_2'$). $r \textbf{e}_1$ represents some displacement $r$ along the $\textbf{e}_1$ direction ($\textbf{e}_1$ is the unit vector along the streamwise direction). Finally $L_v$ is calculated by integrating $ R_{21}^{'}$ up to a certain distance $\hat{r}$ where $ R_{21}^{'}$ becomes zero. Measuring $\hat{r}$ is not trivial since the nature of $ R_{21}^{'}$ changes with streamwise distance. Figure \ref{fig:R21_SRG38} shows the variation of $ R_{21}^{'}$ along the the centreline ($y=0$, $z=0$), and $2D$ and $5D$ away from the grid SRG38. Closer to the grid, $ R_{21}^{'}$ clearly falls off to zero, while further downstream it does not. Therefore, a fitting function needs to be used. Note that the background correlation shows multiple secondary peaks, the strongest one is shown by the `\textbf{\textcolor{red}{+}}' sign in figure \ref{fig:R21_SRG38}(b). A cutoff twice the peak value was used (as shown by the red dashed line) and the data above the cutoff was used for fitting. A bi-Gaussian function was used for fitting (two separate Gaussian curves were fitted on both sides of the peak). As an example, the fitted curve is shown in figure figure \ref{fig:R21_SRG38}(b) by the black line. Following equation \ref{integral}, $L_v$ was calculated on both the sides and an average of the two was taken. Towards the ends of the FOV where fitting could not be performed on both sides, $L_v$ was estimated simply based on data from one side.} Figure \ref{fig:FST_Lv} shows the variation of integral length scale ($L_v$) along the geometrical centreline. Similar to figure \ref{fig:FST_U}, the filled contour shows transverse variation of $L_v$. The dashed vertical lines show the positions of the turbine in different experiments. 


\rA{The integral length scales could also be obtained using other autocorrelation functions such as $ R_{12}^{'}$ (transverse autocorrelation function of streamwise velocity fluctuation) or $ R_{11}^{'}$ (streamwise autocorrelation function of streamwise velocity fluctuation). Examples of these autocorrelation functions downstream of the grids SRG38 and SFG5 are shown in figure \ref{fig:corr}. Looking at the area under the curves, one can tell that $R_{11}^{'}$ yields much larger integral length scales while $R_{21}^{'}$ and $R_{12}^{'}$ yields integral length scales of similar size.} 




