\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:Conclusion}

The experiment of polarized deuteron scattering on various heavy target aims to investigate the isovector reorientation (IVR) effect and its inferrence of the stiffness of nuclear symmetry energy at sub-saturation density. The experiment is proposed to run at the SAMURAI spectrometer at RIKEN, utilizing a polarized deuteron beam with an energy of 190 MeV/u. In this paper, we demonstrate the method to monitor the $p_{z'z'}$ polarization and verify the effectiveness of $p_{y'y'}$ polarization monitoring proposed by previous studies. We have designed the detector setup and optimized the target position to maximize the detection efficiency for both neutrons and protons. The responses of the detectors to these particles were thoroughly analyzed, along with simulations of the background and the beam dumper to minimize the interference from background. It is found that, the IVR effect, measured by the observable $R$, is more pronounced in neutron rich target $^{124}$Sn than in neutron deficient target $^{112}$Sn, and more pronounced in  $^{124}$Sn  than in  $^{208}$Pb. Moreover, the $z'$-polarized beam is more favored than the $y'$-polarized beam.  In all cases, the IVR effect can be clearly observed in the simulations.  The results demonstrate the feasibility  of the experiment.

%The findings of this study establish a robust experimental methodology for probing the isovector nuclear potential and provide stringent constraints on the density dependence of the symmetry energy, a fundamental parameter in nuclear structure and reaction dynamics. These results have significant implications for astrophysics, particularly in modeling neutron stars and supernovae, and pave the way for future investigations with improved detector configurations and broader experimental coverage. Furthermore, precise knowledge of the symmetry energy has far-reaching implications in related fields such as astrophysics, where it plays a crucial role in understanding the properties of neutron stars and the dynamics of supernova explosions. 
