\begin{abstract}
Multiport interferometers can be constructed out of two-port components in various ways. We study how this choice affects the quality of the operations performed by the interferometer, through the asymmetry of optical losses in the multiport. We find that some operations benefit from asymmetrically designed interferometers. Our results point to a fundamental tradeoff between lowering infidelity and achieving high success rates, which are key considerations in quantum information processing applications. Our results challenge the common understanding that balanced losses are always optimal for quantum information processing tasks.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Integrated linear optical circuits form the backbone of scalable photonic quantum information processing technologies \cite{carolan2015universal,adcock2019programmable,llewellyn2020chip,li2022quantum,moody2022,pai2023experimental,huang2024demonstration,hoch2025quantum,li2025realizing,salavrakos2025photon,psiquantum2025manufacturable}.
The miniaturization of optical components such as beam splitters and phase shifters makes it possible to build compact, large-scale interferometric networks. These multiport interferometers enable precise control over quantum states of light, while their implementation in the form of photonic integrated circuits (PICs) provides strong resilience to environmental noise (e.g., vibrations and temperature fluctuations), which is essential for achieving high-fidelity and coherent quantum state manipulation \cite{miller1969integrated}.

It is well established that any arbitrary unitary transformation acting on a set of optical modes can be exactly decomposed into a sequence of unitary operations, each confined to a two-mode subspace, which aligns naturally with the capabilities of linear optical components in quantum photonic systems \cite{nielsen2010quantum,reck1994experimental,clements2016optimal}. Two seemingly distinct multiport interferometer design strategies have emerged from this framework: the \textit{triangular} design (introduced by Reck \textit{et al.} \cite{reck1994experimental}) and the \textit{rectangular} design (introduced by Clements \textit{et al.} \cite{clements2016optimal}), which differ in optical depth and loss asymmetry. Both designs can be understood as special cases within a unified framework of interferometer designs that are interchangeable through systematic transformations \cite{vandebril2011chasing,clement2022lov}. 

However, to realize universal photonic quantum information processing capacity, linear optical circuits need projective measurements on one or more of the optical output modes, as well as feedforward, effectively introducing the nonlinearity required for implementing entangling gates and conditional operations \cite{knill2001scheme}. This measurement-induced nonlinearity motivates classification of linear optical quantum operations into two categories (Fig. \ref{fig:classification}), of which we will address several examples: a) \textit{full-measurement} operations, where all output modes are measured, typically used in tasks such as boson sampling \cite{aaronson2011computational,brod2019photonic}, and b) \textit{partial-measurement} operations, where only a subset of modes is measured, leaving the quantum information encoded in the remaining modes intact for further processing.  
In the latter case, post-selection on specific measurement results is often used to herald successful operations, such as in the generation of entangled states \cite{forbes2025heralded} (e.g., Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) states \cite{gubarev2020improved}) or in photon distillation \cite{marshall2022distillation, somhorst2024photon, saied2024general}. 

\begin{figure}
    \centering
     \includegraphics[width=8.6cm, height=20cm, keepaspectratio,]{ConceptualClassification.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Classification of linear optical quantum operations.} A quantum channel mapping $\rho \rightarrow \mathcal{T}(\rho)$ is implemented through a multiport interferometer that executes a linear transformation $T$ on the creation operators, followed by projective measurements. A) \textit{Full-measurement} operation where all output modes are simultaneously measured, yielding classical information as the main output. B) \textit{Partial-measurement} operation where one or more output modes are simultaneously measured. Postselection on specific outcomes of the classical measurement results yields a heralded state $\rho^\prime$, which constitutes the conditional quantum information output of the circuit.}
    \label{fig:classification}
\end{figure}

Conventionally, circuit design optimization focuses exclusively on maximizing circuit success probability under ideal conditions \cite{gubarev2020improved}. However, realistic imperfections, such as photon loss, transformation matrix infidelity, and photonic indistinguishability errors, directly degrade the fidelity of the feedforward information \cite{flamini2017benchmarking,shaw2023errors,saied2024advancing}, even with established low-loss PIC technology \cite{chanana2022ultra,taballione20198,taballione202320}. Therefore, maximizing the circuit success probability may not necessarily lead to the lowest quantum operation loss in practice.

The rectangular multiport interferometer is widely regarded as the most effective design strategy to mitigate the impact of photon loss \cite{pai2023experimental,huang2024demonstration,hoch2025quantum,li2025realizing,salavrakos2025photon,zhou2022photonic,erhard2020advances,bai2023photonic,bandyopadhyay2024single,kitayama2019novel}. Its inherent symmetry in photon loss preserves the target interference pattern, subject only to a uniform attenuation factor upon postselection \cite{clements2016optimal}. Furthermore, for certain tasks, such as Bell state measurements, symmetric loss minimizes the attenuation of the overall success rate \cite{hilaire2023linear}. Conversely, asymmetric losses often lead to sampling bias, which is believed to negatively affect the fidelity of the measured quantum information. However, this intuition is not always true, as demonstrated in the context of Bell inequality violation tasks \cite{giustina2013bell}. Certain Bell inequalities, such as the Eberhard inequality \cite{eberhard1993background}, have been designed to accommodate asymmetric detection efficiencies in, for instance, atom-photon entangled systems \cite{brunner2007detection}. 

In this work, we numerically study how the distribution of photon losses affects different quantum operations. We introduce metrics for the success of full-measurement and partial-measurement operations, and evaluate these metrics for a variety of linear optical tasks. For the examples studied in this work, we find that for full-measurement tasks, an unbalanced design outperforms a balanced one unless postselection on photon number preservation is performed, while in the partial-measurement case, the unbalanced design outperforms the balanced one in the quality of the produced state. Our findings demonstrate that partial-measurement operations gain advantages from asymmetric multiport interferometer designs, challenging the prevailing belief that symmetric configurations are superior.

The paper is structured as follows. First, we present a brief review of the framework for linear optical transformations and extend it to include losses. We introduce essential metrics for assessing the efficiency of quantum operations. Subsequently, we analyze the effect of postselection on the performance of multiport interferometers within the context of boson sampling. Finally, we illustrate the advantages of asymmetric loss distributions by evaluating both rectangular and triangular multiport interferometer configurations in quantum state engineering applications, in particular photon distillation and the generation of 3-qubit GHZ states. 

\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:preliminaries}

\subsection{Linear transformations in lossy systems}
We begin with a concise introduction to unitary transformations within the framework of linear quantum optics, and we extend this framework to include non-unitary transformations. Fig. \ref{fig:classification} illustrates that a linear optical quantum operation $\mathcal{T}(\rho)$ is composed of a multiport interferometer and projective measurement devices \footnote{In our context, $\rho$ may represent a state that is part of a larger entangled state, as seen in fusion gates \cite{bartolucci2023fusion}.}. Thus, the interference part of any quantum operation is fully characterized by a transformation matrix $T$, which describes the behavior of a lossy multiport interferometer at the system level. 

Without photon losses, the multiport interferometer executes a unitary operation $\mathcal{U}$, which results in the input state being transformed as $\rho \rightarrow \mathcal{U} \rho \mathcal{U}^\dagger$. However, linear optical elements such as beam splitters and phase shifters act on the modes of the quantized electromagnetic field, making it more practical to express the unitary operator as a transformation of the bosonic creation operators: 
\begin{equation}
       \hat{a}_j^\dagger \rightarrow \sum_{i = 1} U_{ij} \hat{a}_i^\dagger,
\end{equation}
where the subscript $i$ ($j$) indicates the output (input) port label, and $U$ is a unitary matrix. 

In the presence of photon losses, the multiport interferometer executes a non-unitary operation expressed by the Kraus operators $E_k$ as $\rho \rightarrow \sum_k E_k\rho E_k^\dagger$. Analogous to the lossless scenario, this non-unitary operation is represented by a transformation matrix $T$ that operates on the creation operators:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{a}_j^\dagger \rightarrow \sum_{i = 1} T_{ij} \hat{a}_i^\dagger,
\end{equation}
where $T^\dagger T \leq \mathcal{I}$.

The sub-unitary matrix $T$ can be viewed as a submatrix of a larger unitary matrix $\tilde{U}$, with $T$ capturing only the coupling between measurable (‘\textit{nominal}’) input and output modes \cite{tischler2018quantum}. In contrast, the purified transformation $\tilde{U}$ also captures the coupling to unmeasurable (‘\textit{virtual}’) modes, representing losses. Given a lossy transformation $T$, a purified transformation $\tilde{U}$ can be easily constructed using the methods described in Ref. \cite{tischler2018quantum}. This fact provides a convenient framework for analyzing the effects of loss on quantum operations, because accounting for photons that are lost to virtual modes offers an effective strategy for tracking all photons. We adopt this computational method throughout our work. 


\subsection{Emergence of lossy transformations}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
