    \label{fig:design}
\end{figure}

Next, we will explain how the choice of interferometer design influences $T$. In practice, any multiport interferometer can be constructed by arranging a series of universal two-port interferometers, or \textit{unit cells}, in a network-like configuration (Fig. \ref{fig:design}). Out of all universal interchangeable network-like configurations, the triangular \cite{reck1994experimental} and rectangular \cite{clements2016optimal} designs are the best known, but intermediate designs are readily achieved by implementing a given set of transformation rules \cite{vandebril2011chasing,clement2022lov}. In theory, all universal interferometer designs are equivalent. However, designs vary in how they handle photon losses, which results in different $T$-matrices. In this work, we restrict our analysis to rectangular and triangular designs, as they represent the 'extreme values' across the spectrum of photon loss distributions within interferometers.

Throughout our work, we adopt the convention that each unit cell is characterized by a uniform single-photon transmission efficiency $\eta$ because of balanced propagation losses \cite{melkozerov2024analysis}. Unit cells are easily constructed using a configurable Mach-Zehnder interferometer (MZI) complemented by a single-mode phase shifter, but more compact unit cell designs exist \cite{bell2021further}. Regardless of the exact unit cell design, the internal physical path lengths must be matched within the photon coherence length to enable interference \cite{clements2016optimal}, which motivates the assumption of balanced propagation losses.

We use the lumped element models in Fig. \ref{fig:design} to better understand how the design-dependent $T$-matrix emerges. In these models, lossy two-port unit cells are decomposed as two single-mode loss channels with uniform transmission efficiency $\eta$, followed by a two-mode unitary transformation. Every loss channel is represented by a beam splitter interaction with a virtual mode, leading to a linear transformation
\begin{equation}
    \hat{a}_j^\dagger \rightarrow \sqrt{\eta} \hat{a}_j^\dagger + \sqrt{1-\eta}\hat{v}_j^\dagger,
    \label{eq:LosschannelBS}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{v}_j^\dagger$ is the creation operator of a distinct virtual mode that remains inaccessible. In essence, we can view the loss channel as an amplitude-damping error, given that $0 \leq \eta \leq 1$. In contrast to Refs. \cite{reck1994experimental,clements2016optimal}, we assign loss channels to single-port identity transformations since they experience similar on-chip propagation losses. However, we disregard all loss channels that do not depend on the configuration of the unit cell network (e.g., source, coupling, and detection losses), as the scope of this work is confined to studying the impact of photon loss distributions that arise from multiport interferometer design choices.

Fig. \ref{fig:design} illustrates how a linear optical circuit may exhibit significant asymmetric losses, even when its local components have symmetric losses. Given that symmetric losses commute with linear optical transformations \cite{oszmaniec2018classical,brod2020classical}, it is possible to shift some loss channels to the input ports, offering substantial insights into the overall loss distribution. We capture the overall loss distribution in a matrix $\Gamma$, such that $|\Gamma_{ij}|^2$ quantifies the single-photon transmittance between each pair $(i,j)$ of output/input ports. Evidently, the configuration of $\Gamma$ is defined by the chosen interferometer design. For an $m$-port rectangular design, this configuration is straightforward and the matrix elements are defined by:
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma_{ij} = \sqrt{\eta}^m.
    \label{eq:Gamma_rectangular}
\end{equation} 
For a triangular design, however, the configuration is defined by:
\begin{eqnarray}
  \Gamma_{ij} := \begin{cases}
    \sqrt{\eta}^{1 + 2m -i -j} &2\leq i,j \leq m,\\
    \Gamma_{i,2} &2 \leq i \leq m,j = 1,\\
    \Gamma_{2,j} &i = 1, 2 \leq j \leq m,\\
    \Gamma_{2,2} & i = 1, j = 1.
  \end{cases}
  \label{eq:Gamma_triangular}
\end{eqnarray}
This difference explains how the multiport interferometer design choice influences $T$. As the matrix elements $\Gamma_{ij}$ effectively characterize the overall amplitude-damping error for each input-output channel akin to Eq. \ref{eq:LosschannelBS}, reconstructing any lossy $T$-matrix involves combining a specified unitary transformation matrix $U$ with the design-specific loss matrix $\Gamma$:
\begin{equation}
    T = \Gamma \odot U,
    \label{eq:LossyT}
\end{equation}
where $\odot$ is the element-wise matrix product ($(T_{ij} = \Gamma_{ij}U_{ij}$). Thus, the dependency of $\Gamma$ on the design implies a similar dependency of $T$. In the course of this work, Eq. \ref{eq:LossyT} and both $\Gamma$ definitions for rectangular and triangular designs are used to compute the lossy $T$-matrix for a particular unitary matrix $U$ that is of interest.

\subsection{Performance of lossy transformations}
Up to this point, we have established that the effect of losses on a quantum operation can be represented by a sub-unitary $T$-matrix. We have outlined how this $T$-matrix is defined and how it relates to the specific layout of the underlying multiport interferometer. To evaluate and compare the performance of various designs implementing a given partial-measurement quantum operation, we now present two figures of merit as evaluation tools:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Circuit fidelity
    \item Conditional transmittance
\end{itemize}

First, we note that the circuit fidelity is often benchmarked by the normalized average fidelity of the single-photon state transformations \cite{clements2016optimal,flamini2017benchmarking}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{F}(U,T) = \left|\frac{\tr{U^\dagger T}}{\sqrt{\tr{U^\dagger U}  \tr{T^\dagger T}}} \right|^2.
    \label{eq:Fidelity_Clements}
\end{equation}

This definition implicitly assumes postselection on full transmission of all injected photons. However, in many full-measurement quantum information schemes, such as boson sampling, such an assumption is inappropriate, either because full transmission is technologically not achievable \cite{wang2019boson}, or because the photon number at the input of the interferometer is indeterminate \cite{zhong2020quantum,zhong2021phase,madsen2022quantum,deng2023gaussian}. Moreover, in partial-measurement quantum operations, unmeasured output modes are more critical as they carry quantum information and remain unmeasured during operation. Therefore, we shift our focus from the \textit{input} to the \textit{output} single-photon states. Specifically, we study the \textit{preimage} fidelity of states occupying a single output mode to highlight the impact of photon losses on modes sensitive to quantum channel loss. In this context, the preimage of the single-photon state $\ket{\psi_i} := \hat{a}_i^\dagger \ket{0}$ which occupies output mode $i$, under the transformation $M$, is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \ket{\psi_i(M)} = \sum_{j}^{m}M_{ij}\hat{a}_j^\dagger \ket{0}. 
\end{equation}
Therefore, the preimage fidelity of a lossy linear optical circuit is given by:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\mathcal{F}}_i=|\braket{\psi_i(U)|\psi_i(T)}|^2 = |\sum_{j}^{m}U_{ij}^\dagger T_{ij}|^2,
    \label{eq:Fidelity_Preimage}
\end{equation}
and we define the average preimage fidelity as:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\mathcal{F}}_{\text{avg}} := \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i}^{m}|\sum_{j}^{m}U_{ij}^\dagger T_{ij}|^2.
\end{equation}
As the transformations of both single-photon and multiphoton states depend on the matrix elements of $T$, these circuit fidelities provide indirectly valuable heuristic insights into predicting the performance of quantum operations that involve multiple photons.

A conventional direct figure of merit for evaluating partial-measurement quantum operations is the circuit success probability $p_s$. The circuit success probability is often conditioned on detecting a certain number of photons in each measured mode, such that a successful event indicates the projection onto a specific quantum state $\rho^\prime$. However, the circuit success probability does not fully reflect the successful transmission of quantum information.
Even when a measurement indicates success, the output state $\rho^\prime$ remains susceptible to vacuum errors. Therefore, we introduce and focus in this work on a conditional transmittance measure 
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda := \tr{\rho^\prime (\mathcal{I} - \ket{0}\bra{0})},
    \label{eq:FidelityConditionel}
\end{equation}
where $0 \leq \Lambda \leq 1$. In other words, $1-\Lambda$ represents the probability that the heralded state is empty, i.e., $\rho^\prime = \ket{0}\bra{0}$. In the context of this work, ideally $\Lambda = 1$, which is satisfied given $T = U$. 

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Boson Sampling}
Boson sampling is a task formulated to demonstrate an unambiguous quantum computational advantage \cite{aaronson2011computational,brod2019photonic}. This quantum operation consists of sampling the output distribution of indistinguishable photons after they passed through a multiport interferometer, categorizing it as a full-measurement operation. It is crucial that the matrix transformations $U$ implemented correspond to Haar-random matrices \cite{mezzadri2006generate}, therefore circuit fidelity serves as a key metric in evaluating boson sampling experiments \cite{clements2016optimal}. 

We concentrate on the non-postselected preimage fidelity (Eq. \ref{eq:Fidelity_Preimage}) to assess the total impact of photon losses on quantum operations. Fig. \ref{fig:ReimageFidelity} shows our results for the preimage fidelity of different 5-mode multiport interferometer designs as a function of varying single-photon transmission efficiency for Haar-random $U$. We observe that the average fidelity of the triangular design is consistently higher than that of the rectangular design. However, not all modes perform similarly within the triangular design because of intrinsic asymmetric losses. Therefore, we also present results for the output modes with minimum fidelity ($i=1,2$) and maximum fidelity ($i = 5$). Our results indicate that the common assumption that rectangular designs are preferred over triangular designs is a consequence of postselection bias in Eq. \ref{eq:Fidelity_Clements}.  

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
     \includegraphics[width=8.6cm, height=20cm, keepaspectratio,]{ReimageFidelity_20250417.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Impact of loss on 5-mode multiport interferometers.} The non-postselected single-photon preimage fidelity, $\tilde{\mathcal{F}}$, is shown as a function of the single-photon transmission efficiency per unit cell, $\eta$. On average, the triangular circuit design exhibits greater robustness to losses than the rectangular design. However, while the rectangular design shows no variation in the fidelity of any specific output mode, the triangular design shows significant variability, as evidenced by the spread between the maximum and minimum preimage fidelity. Each data point corresponds to an average over 500 Haar-random unitary matrix samples. Inset: Detailed view of the high-efficiency regime. }
    \label{fig:ReimageFidelity}
\end{figure}

In the context of partial-measurement quantum operations, these results highlight that directing quantum information to the highest-fidelity output mode reduces vulnerability to quantum operation infidelity. This conclusion is directly supported by Fig. \ref{fig:design}: if we trace the path of the photon emerging in output mode 5 back to the input modes, we see that this photon encounters fewer lossy elements in the triangular design than in the rectangular design, making it less susceptible to photon losses. In contrast to Ref. \cite{clements2016optimal}, we also assigned propagation losses to single-mode identity transformations in the boundary paths. In App. \ref{app:LTmultiport} we discuss how including single-mode identity transformation losses leads to slightly better postselected circuit fidelities compared to the results presented in Ref. \cite{clements2016optimal}.

\subsection{Photon distillation}
Photon distillation is a scalable quantum error mitigation protocol that helps to create highly identical single-photon states, thereby reducing resource costs in photonic quantum computing architectures \cite{somhorst2024photon,saied2024general}. This quantum operation consists of creating a higher quality photon from multiple, lower quality photons through multiphoton interference, categorizing it as a partial-measurement operation. Photon-distillation circuits inherently reduce errors related to photonic indistinguishability, but this additional component leads
to an increase in photon loss errors. Yet, initial tradeoff assessments lean towards the adoption of photon distillation \cite{somhorst2024photon}, which can be further enhanced by minimizing inevitable photon losses. Therefore, the conditional transmittance is the key metric in evaluating photon distillation circuit designs.  

The $U$ matrix relevant to photon distillation is the $N-$mode Fourier transform, defined by the matrix elements $U_{jk} := \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\exp(i\frac{2\pi(j-1)(k-1)}{N})$. Unlike typical partial-measurement circuits, a successfully completed photon-distillation task produces an unentangled state confined to only \textit{one} output mode (not being the vacuum state). Since the intended multiphoton interference is affected by asymmetric losses, the success probability \textit{distribution} is changed non-trivially. However, this asymmetric loss reduces a \textit{given} sample probability similarly for identical and non-identical photons. Consequently, the target reduction in indistinguishability error is independent of the design-dependent $T$-matrix. 

We find that the conditional transmittance induced by rectangular and triangular designs scales differently with respect to the unit cell single-photon transmission efficiency $\eta$ and the size of the distillation circuit $N$. In particular, we find for the rectangular design:
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda_{\text{rectangular}} = \eta^N,
    \label{eq:Lambda_rectangular}
\end{equation}
while we find for the triangular design: 
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda_{\text{triangular}} = \frac{N(1-\eta)}{2\eta - \eta^2 - \eta^N}\eta^N.
    \label{eq:Lambda_triangular}
\end{equation}
These results can be understood by considering the lossless circuit success probability \cite{somhorst2024photon}: 
\begin{equation}
    p_0 = \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} (-1)^j (j+1) \prod_{i = 1}^{j} \left(1-\frac{i}{N}\right) + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
    \label{eq:p_s_lossless}
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon$ parametrizes the indistinguishability error. 
Within this limit, photon-distillation operations are naturally resilient to loss, since the fundamental zero transmission law prevents an incorrect $N$-photon distribution across the measured modes from becoming a valid $N-1$ photon herald measurement \cite{tichy2010zero}. Consequently, assuming a rectangular multiport interferometer design, the impact of loss on photon-distillation tasks becomes readily apparent: the circuit success probability is given by 
\begin{equation}
    p_{\text{s,rectangular}} = p_0\eta^{N(N-1)} + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
    \label{eq:PD_Clements_p_s}
\end{equation}
and the conditional transmittance is set by
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda_{\text{rectangular}} = \eta^{N} + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon).
\end{equation}

Intuitively, this understanding can also be gained by examining Fig. \ref{fig:design} A: all losses are commuted to the input, allowing the interferometer to function as if it were lossless. The probability that $N-1$ auxiliary photons will survive is $(\eta^N)^{N-1}$, while the survival probability for the remaining $N^{\text{th}}$ photon is simply given by $\eta^N$. 


Unfortunately, Fig. \ref{fig:design} B indicates that applying a similar approach to the triangular design is unsuccessful, because not all losses can be commuted to the input. However, the 'cleaned' design reveals that the path towards output mode $N$ is free of loss. Given that $U$ is balanced, each input mode has a probability of $\frac{1}{N}$ of being the source of the distilled photon. Therefore, the circuit success probability $p_{\text{s,triangular}}$ is found by averaging over all combinations of tracing back the input losses of the distilled photon, in other words, this statement implies:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    p_{\text{s,triangular}} &= \frac{1}{N} \left ( \eta^{-(N-1)} + \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \eta^{-i} \right )p_{\text{s,triangular}}\Lambda_{\text{triangular}} \\&+ \mathcal{O}(\epsilon),
\end{split}
\end{equation}
which implies (after simplifications):
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda_{\text{triangular}} = \frac{N(1-\eta)\eta^N}{2\eta - \eta^2 - \eta^N} + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon).
    \label{eq:p_erasure_reck}
\end{equation}

Fig. \ref{fig:PD_N_5_transmittance} demonstrates the impact of switching multiport interferometer designs for the ($N$=5)-photon Fourier transform-based distillation circuit. Here, we plotted the conditional transmittance for different values of $\eta$. We observe that in the regime $0 < \eta < 1$, the triangular scheme demonstrates a higher conditional transmittance, indicating better loss-tolerance properties. Overall, a comparison between Eqs. \ref{eq:Lambda_rectangular} and \ref{eq:Lambda_triangular} reveals that shifting from rectangular to triangular configurations becomes more beneficial as the size of photon-distillation circuits increases due to the prefactor $N$ in Eq. \ref{eq:Lambda_triangular}.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=8.6cm, height=20cm, keepaspectratio,]{PD_transmittance.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Impact of loss on the ($N$=5)-photon Fourier transform-based distillation circuit.} 
    The conditional transmittance, $\Lambda$, is shown as a function of unit cell transmission efficiency $\eta$ for both rectangular and triangular designs. The triangular design consistently yields higher conditional transmittance than the rectangular design except for $\eta \in \{0,1\}$.}
    \label{fig:PD_N_5_transmittance}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger states}
The generation of GHZ states is a method used to produce highly entangled quantum states, with the 3-qubit version (GHZ-3) identified as the key component for developing scalable and fault-tolerant architectures in photonic quantum computing \cite{varnava2008good,bartolucci2023fusion,chen2024heralded}. This quantum operation consists of entangling three photons with the help of projective measurements on auxiliary photons and multiphoton interference, categorizing it as a partial-measurement operation \cite{gubarev2020improved}. It is essential that the multiport interferometer introduces minimal additional losses to the individual qubit loss rates in order to support fault-tolerant operation, therefore the conditional transmittance of each individual qubit is the key metric in evaluating GHZ-state generation circuit designs. 

One of the first successful demonstrations of on-chip heralded 3-qubit GHZ state generation has recently been achieved experimentally by Chen \textit{et al.} \cite{chen2024heralded}. Yet, the total heralding efficiency of $\sim 0.0005$ poses significant scaling challenges. The on-chip unit cell efficiency of the rectangular multiport interferometer is estimated to be $\eta = 0.9848$ (App. \ref{app:GHZ3}), indicating that there is potential for improvement through changes in circuit design.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=8.6cm, height=20cm, keepaspectratio,]{QubitErasureV4.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Conditional transmittance of qubits within heralded 3-qubit GHZ states.} Despite the symmetric losses in the rectangular design, the marginal conditional qubit transmittance $\Lambda$ remains nonuniform. Interestingly, the triangular design mitigates this non-uniformity of conditional transmittance, particularly in qubits 2 and 3. Marginal conditional transmittance is calculated for $\eta = 0.9848$.}
    \label{fig:GHZ3_qubiterasure}
\end{figure}

While significant progress is needed to increase the total heralding efficiency, a triangular multiport interferometer design offers a modest improvement. 
Fig. \ref{fig:GHZ3_qubiterasure} reveals that the conditional qubit transmittance within a GHZ-3 state is non-uniformly distributed, even though the underlying rectangular circuit exhibits symmetric losses. This non-uniformity is attributed to events involving more than three photons in the measured modes, which non-trivially map onto the postselected three-photon herald measurement due to losses (App. \ref{app:GHZ3}). Alternatively, Fig. \ref{fig:GHZ3_qubiterasure} shows that by transitioning to a triangular design, the conditional qubit transmittance is improved for all qubits. Counterintuitively, the transmittance of qubits 2 and 3 shows a closer similarity despite the underlying asymmetric losses. However, this improvement comes at the expense of a slight increase in stabilizer measurement errors (App. \ref{app:GHZ3}).

\section{Discussion}
In summary, we have demonstrated that asymmetric multiport interferometer designs offer greater advantages over symmetric ones for implementing partial-measurement quantum operations. We explicitly showed that the vacuum error component in the output of photon distillation and GHZ-state generation circuits is reduced by adopting a triangular instead of a rectangular multiport interferometer design, although this occurs at the cost of a decreased probability of circuit success. However, this decrease can be compensated for by multiplexing \cite{migdall2002tailoring}, which adds a constant overhead to each quantum information processing task. 

Our work opens the question of whether balanced or unbalanced losses are optimal for other quantum optical circuits, and provides the methodology to resolve this question. Two further research directions are whether the interferometers that interpolate between triangular and rectangular offer superior performance to both, and to provide a general criterion that predicts whether balanced or unbalanced losses are optimal without a detailed calculation of each specific case.

To achieve scalable and fault-tolerant quantum computing using linear optics, it is essential that photonic integrated circuits produce independently degraded entangled states, i.e., states where each qubit shares an independent and uniform loss probability \cite{varnava2008good}. However, our results challenge the commonly assumed link between symmetric multiport interferometer designs and uniform qubit losses. We expect that our work contributes to a better understanding of how photon loss distributions in circuit design lead to losses at the qubit level.  

