\section{Impact of single-mode identity transformation losses}
\label{app:LTmultiport}
We closely follow the method described in Ref. \cite{clements2016optimal} to assess the resilience of multiport interferometers to unbalanced losses. For $N = 20$, we generate 500 Haar-random unitary matrices $U$. We then introduce a variable uniform loss $l$ [dB] at the input modes of each two-mode \textit{and single-mode} transformation, that results in a single-photon transmission efficiency $\eta = 10^{-l/10}$. Next, we use the definitions in Eqs. \ref{eq:Gamma_rectangular} and \ref{eq:Gamma_triangular} to find $\Gamma$ for the 
rectangular and triangular design. Subsequently, we recompile the lossy transformation matrices $T$ via Eq. \ref{eq:LossyT}. We quantify the postselected fidelity by evaluating Eq. \ref{eq:Fidelity_Clements}. 

Fig. \ref{fig:FidelityInterferometer} shows our numerical simulation results for a 20-mode multiport interferometer with varying loss, and the results of Ref. \cite{clements2016optimal}. We observe within both data sets that the triangular design's fidelity significantly declines with increasing losses, while the rectangular design maintains its fidelity. The asymmetric losses inherent in the triangular design completely account for this observation. By comparing our data to the data of Ref. \cite{clements2016optimal}, we observe that by including the loss resulting from single-mode transformations in the triangular (rectangular) design, the asymmetric loss slightly increases (vanishes), and therefore the fidelity is slightly reduced (restored). 

