\section{Loss-tolerance of triangular GHZ-state generating circuits}
\label{app:GHZ3}
We examine the tolerance to loss in GHZ state generating circuits by concentrating on the heralded linear optical circuit used by Chen \textit{et al.} for generating a photonic dual-rail encoded GHZ-3 state \cite{chen2024heralded}. A low-loss 12-mode universal rectangular multiport interferometer is used to program the circuit of Gubarev \textit{et al.} \cite{gubarev2020improved}, with a total on-chip propagation loss budget of 0.8 dB \cite{taballione2021universal}. This propagation loss yields a standard unit cell transmission efficiency $\eta$, calculated as $\eta = 10^{-\frac{0.8/12}{10}} = 0.9848$. 
\
Gubarev \textit{et al.} designed a 10-mode unitary matrix transformation $U$ that necessitates a total of six photons, comprising three photons for the dual-rail encoded qubits and three auxiliary photons for the measurement-induced circuit nonlinearity. We arrange input and output permutations to heuristically minimize the impact of asymmetric losses in the triangular design. This configuration ensures that the six photons enter through input modes 6 to 10, while successful operation is confirmed by detecting auxiliary photons at output modes 1, 3, and 4. Qubits $Q_1$, $Q_2$, and $Q_3$ are associated with output mode pairs $(5,6)$, $(7,8)$, and $(9,10)$, respectively. The relevant submatrix of the adjusted transformation matrix is given by:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    U_{\text{sub}} &:= \\
    &\left [ \begin{smallmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0.3536 & -0.3536 & -0.3536 & 0.3536 \\
0 & 0 & 0.3536 & 0.3536 & 0.3536 & 0.3536 \\
0.4082 & 0.4082 & 0.2887 & 0.2887 & -0.2887 & -0.2887 \\
0.4082 & -0.4082 & 0.2887 & -0.2887 & 0.2887 & -0.2887 \\
-0.8165 & 0 & 0.2887 & 0 & 0 & -0.2887 \\
0 & 0 & 0.5000 & 0 & 0 & -0.5000 \\
0 & 0 & 0.5000 & 0 & 0 & 0.5000 \\
0 & 0.8165 & 0 & -0.2887 & 0.2887 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & -0.5000 & 0.5000 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0.5000 & 0.5000 & 0 \\
\end{smallmatrix} \right ],
\end{split}
\label{eq:U_sub_matrix}
\end{equation}
where columns correspond to input modes and rows correspond to output modes. An important observation from Eq. \ref{eq:U_sub_matrix} is that a nonuniversal interferometer suffices to implement the scheme of Ref. \cite{gubarev2020improved}, since there is no all-to-all connectivity. For example, a photon entering through input mode six will not contribute to $Q_3$. Consequently, we anticipate that there is potential for further enhancements to the circuit design beyond the triangular design studied in this work. 

One potential downside of switching multiport interferometer designs, however, is the emergence of stabilizer measurement errors. Since the triangular design does not preserve the target interference \cite{clements2016optimal}, we expect a post-selected state infidelity error.  Given that state infidelity indicates that the postselected heralded three-qubit state $\rho^\prime$ deviates from the stabilized state $\ket{\text{GHZ}_3}\bra{\text{GHZ}_3}$, it becomes essential to quantify the resulting implications \cite{saied2024advancing,shaw2023errors}. We quantify the stabilizer errors by evaluating the expectation values to calculate the stabilizer measurement error:
\begin{equation}
    p_{\text{error}} := \frac{1}{2}\left (1-\langle S\rangle \right),
\end{equation}
where the expectation value of the stabilizer $S$ is given by $\langle S \rangle = \tr{\rho^\prime S}$, $-1 \leq \langle S \rangle \leq 1$ and $\langle S \rangle = +1$ if $\rho^\prime = \ket{\text{GHZ}_3}\bra{\text{GHZ}_3}$. 

Fig. \ref{fig:GHZ3_stabilizererror} shows the penalty in stabilizer errors for transitioning to a triangular multiport interferometer design. Certain stabilizers experience a measurement error of $p_{\text{error}} = 0.013\%$, while others remain unaffected. 

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=8.6cm, height=20cm, keepaspectratio,]{StabilizerErrorV2.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Stabilizer measurement errors of the postselected heralded 3-qubit GHZ states.} The underlying 10-mode interferometer is built according to the triangular multiport interferometer design, assuming $\eta = 0.9848$. Averaging the stabilizer errors yields a state infidelity $1 - f = 1.3 \times 10^{-4}$. }
    \label{fig:GHZ3_stabilizererror}
\end{figure}

In fact, the post-selected heralded three-qubit state remains pure in the presence of asymmetric losses and is parametrized by:
\begin{equation}
    \ket{\psi^\prime} = \sin{\alpha} \ket{000} + \cos{\alpha} \ket{111}, 
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ determines the post-selected state fidelity:
\begin{equation}
    f = |\braket{\text{GHZ}_3 | \psi^\prime}|^2 = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2}\sin{2 \alpha}. 
\end{equation}
For $\eta = 0.9848$, we compute $\alpha = 0.7969$ and $1 - f = 1.3 \times 10^{-4}$. 

Fig. \ref{fig:FidelityGHZ3} illustrates the effect of loss on the post-selected state infidelity for the triangular design. We observe that the infidelity is approximately proportional to the unit cell loss squared. Consequently, we infer that the post-selected state infidelity (and therefore the emergence of stabilizer errors) caused by asymmetric losses in the triangular design is only a second-order effect.

