Another critical use case is gene expression imputation. Accurate imputation is crucial in integrative analyses, where maximizing the use of available data enhances statistical power and biological insight. Due to differing gene coverage across platforms and studies in multi-cohort analyses, restricting analyses to shared genes results in information loss. HR-VILAGE-3K3M ’s unique missingness patterns, including genes absent in subsets of studies, make it an ideal benchmark for evaluating and comparing gene imputation methods.

Single-cell data offer a complementary and high-resolution option for investigating immune responses. Unlike bulk data, it enables cell–type–specific analysis, allowing researchers to track how individual cell populations respond to perturbations such as vaccination or infection. This resolution is critical for understanding the heterogeneity and coordination of the cell community itself and together of immune responses over time. Additionally, cell–cell communication dynamics, transcription factor activity inference with gene regulatory network, and lineage or trajectory reconstruction to capture differentiation processes can be further explored. When integrated with bulk data, single-cell information can help refine cell type annotations, validate imputed signals, and guide the interpretation of composite bulk-level changes. These applications expand the utility of HR-VILAGE-3K3M as a benchmark for both population-level and single-cell–informed methods in systems immunology.
